":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Frederick III, German Emperor"}],"people":{"Q10873":{"label":"Leo von Caprivi","description":"German Chancellor and Prussian General of the Infantry and Prime Minister (1831–1899)","enwiki":"Leo von Caprivi","summary":"Georg Leo Graf von Caprivi de Caprara de Montecuccoli (English: Count George Leo of Caprivi, Caprara, and Montecuccoli; born Georg Leo von Caprivi; 24 February 1831 – 6 February 1899) was a German general and statesman. He served as the imperial chancellor of the German Empire from March 1890 to October 1894, succeeding longtime chancellor Otto von Bismarck.\nCaprivi was born into a Prussian noble family of Italian origin. After graduating from the Prussian Staff College, he rapidly rose through the ranks and distinguished himself in particular during the Franco-Prussian War, where he served as chief of staff of the X Army Corps.","image":"Graf Leopold von Caprivi cropped2.jpg","birth":1831.1479452054793,"death":1899.0986301369862},"Q150552":{"label":"Franz von Papen","description":"German general staff officer, politician, diplomat, nobleman and Chancellor of Germany (1879–1969)","enwiki":"Franz von Papen","summary":"Franz Joseph Hermann Michael Maria von Papen, Erbsälzer zu Werl und Neuwerk (German: [ˈfʁants fɔn ˈpaːpn̩] ; 29 October 1879 – 2 May 1969) was a German politician, diplomat, army officer, and Prussian nobleman who served as Chancellor of Germany in 1932, and then as Vice-Chancellor under Adolf Hitler from 1933 to 1934. A national conservative and committed monarchist, he is largely remembered for his role in Hitler's rise to power.\nBorn into an influential aristocratic Westphalian family, Papen served in the Prussian Army from 1898 onward and was trained as an officer of the German General Staff. He served as a military attaché in Mexico and the United States from 1913 to 1915, while also covertly organising acts of sabotage in the United States and quietly backing and financing Mexican forces in the Mexican Revolution on behalf of German military intelligence.","image":"Bundesarchiv Bild 183-1988-0113-500, Franz v. Papen (cropped)(2).jpg","birth":1879.8246575342466,"death":1969.331506849315},"Q47906":{"label":"Hermann Göring","description":"German Nazi politician, military leader, and convicted war criminal (1893–1946)","enwiki":"Hermann Göring","summary":"Hermann Wilhelm Göring (or Goering; German: [ˈhɛʁman ˈvɪlhɛlm ˈɡøːʁɪŋ] ; 12 January 1893 – 15 October 1946) was a German politician, aviator, military commander, and convicted war criminal. He was one of the most powerful figures in the Nazi Party, which controlled Germany from 1933 to 1945. He also served as Oberbefehlshaber der Luftwaffe (Supreme Commander of the Air Force), a position he held until the final days of the regime.\nHe was born in Rosenheim, Bavaria.","image":"Hermann Göring - Röhr.jpg","birth":1893.0301369863014,"death":1946.786301369863},"Q57133":{"label":"Kurt von Schleicher","description":"German chancellor (1882-1934)","enwiki":"Kurt von Schleicher","summary":"Kurt Ferdinand Friedrich Hermann von Schleicher (German: [ˈkʊʁt fɔn ˈʃlaɪçɐ] ; 7 April 1882 – 30 June 1934) was a German military officer and the penultimate chancellor of Germany during the Weimar Republic. A rival for power with Adolf Hitler, Schleicher was assassinated by Hitler's Schutzstaffel (SS) during the Night of the Long Knives in 1934.\nSchleicher was born into a military family in Brandenburg an der Havel. Entering the Prussian Army as a lieutenant in 1900, he rose to become a general staff officer in the Railway Department of the German General Staff and served in the General Staff of the Supreme Army Command during World War I. Schleicher served as liaison between the army and the new Weimar Republic during the German Revolution of 1918–1919.","image":"Bundesarchiv Bild 183-B0527-0001-020, Kurt von Schleicher.jpg","birth":1882.2630136986302,"death":1934.4931506849316},"Q58667":{"label":"Otto Braun","description":"German politician (1872-1955)","enwiki":"Otto Braun","summary":"Otto Braun (28 January 1872 – 15 December 1955) was a politician of the Social Democratic Party of Germany (SPD) during the Weimar Republic. From 1920 to 1932, with only two brief interruptions, Braun was Minister President of the Free State of Prussia. The continuity of personnel in high office resulted in a largely stable government in Prussia, in contrast to the sometimes turbulent politics of the Reich. During his term of office, Prussia's public administration was reorganized along democratic lines.","image":"Bundesarchiv Bild 102-10131, Otto Braun.jpg","birth":1872.0737704918033,"death":1955.9534246575342},"Q65255":{"label":"Paul Hirsch","description":"German politician (1868-1940)","enwiki":"Paul Hirsch (politician)","summary":"Paul Hirsch (17 November 1868 – 1 August 1940) was a German politician and a member of the Social Democratic Party who served as Prime Minister of Prussia from 1918 to 1920.","image":"Paul Hirsch um 1920.jpg","birth":1868.877049180328,"death":1940.5819672131147},"Q680674":{"label":"Botho zu Eulenburg","description":"Prussian politician (1831-1912)","enwiki":"Botho zu Eulenburg","summary":"Botho Wendt August Graf zu Eulenburg (31 July 1831 – 5 February 1912) was a Prussian statesman. Throughout the entire German Empire period he was, alongside Albrecht von Roon the only Minister President of Prussia not also to be Chancellor of Germany, with the two offices being practically synonymous.","image":"Botho Wendt zu Eulenburg.jpg","birth":1831.5780821917808,"death":1912.844262295082},"Q69559":{"label":"Gottfried Ludolf Camphausen","description":"German politician (1803-1890)","enwiki":"Gottfried Ludolf Camphausen","summary":"Gottfried Ludolf Camphausen (10 January 1803 – 3 December 1890) was a Rhenish banker and politician who served as a Prime Minister of Prussia.","image":"Camphausen.jpeg","birth":1803.0246575342467,"death":1890.9205479452055},"Q77183":{"label":"Wilhelm Marx","description":"German politician (1863–1946)","enwiki":"Wilhelm Marx","summary":"Wilhelm Marx (15 January 1863 – 5 August 1946) was a German judge, lawyer, and politician who twice served as chancellor of Germany during the Weimar Republic, from 1923 to 1925 and again from 1926 to 1928. He also briefly held the position of Minister-President of Prussia in 1925. A leading figure in the Centre Party, he served as its chairman from 1922 to 1928. With a total tenure of three years and 73 days, he was the longest-serving chancellor of the Weimar Republic.","image":"Reichskanzler Wilhelm Marx (cropped).jpg","birth":1863.0383561643835,"death":1946.5917808219178},"Q8442":{"label":"Otto von Bismarck","description":"German statesman and Chancellor (1815-1898)","enwiki":"Otto von Bismarck","summary":"Otto Eduard Leopold, Prince of Bismarck, Count of Bismarck-Schönhausen, Duke of Lauenburg (1 April 1815 – 30 July 1898), was a German statesman and diplomat who oversaw the unification of Germany and served as its first chancellor from 1871 to 1890. Bismarck's Realpolitik and firm governance earned him the nickname Iron Chancellor (Eiserner Kanzler).\nFrom Junker landowner origins, Bismarck rose rapidly in Prussian politics under King Wilhelm I of Prussia. He served as the Prussian ambassador to Russia and France and in both houses of the Prussian parliament.","image":"Bundesarchiv Bild 146-2005-0057, Otto von Bismarck.jpg","birth":1815.2465753424658,"death":1898.5753424657535},"Q105013":{"label":"Friedrich Wilhelm I of Prussia","description":"king of Prussia (1688-1740)","enwiki":"Frederick William I of Prussia","summary":"Frederick William I (German: Friedrich Wilhelm I.; 14 August 1688 – 31 May 1740), known as the Soldier King (German: Soldatenkönig), was King in Prussia and Elector of Brandenburg from 1713 until his death in 1740, as well as Prince of Neuchâtel. \nBorn in Berlin, he was raised by the Huguenot governess Marthe de Roucoulle. His political awakening occurred during the Great Northern War's plague outbreak in Prussia, leading to his challenge against corruption and inefficiency in government. He initiated reforms, especially in the military, doubling the Prussian Army, and increased the officer corps to 3,000.","image":"Antoine Pesne - König Friedrich Wilhelm I. von Preußen (ca. 1733).jpg","birth":1688.6174863387978,"death":1740.4125683060108},"Q150652":{"label":"Wilhelm I","description":"19th-century German Emperor and King of Prussia (1797–1888)","enwiki":"Wilhelm I","summary":"Wilhelm I (also known in English as William I; German: Wilhelm Friedrich Ludwig; 22 March 1797 – 9 March 1888) was King of Prussia from 1861 and German Emperor from 1871 until his death in 1888. A member of the House of Hohenzollern, he was the first head of state of a united Germany. He was regent of Prussia from 1858 to 1861 for his elder brother, King Frederick William IV. During the reign of his grandson Wilhelm II, he was known as Emperor Wilhelm the Great (German: Kaiser Wilhelm der Große).\nThe second son of Prince Frederick William and Louise of Mecklenburg-Strelitz, Wilhelm was not expected to ascend to the throne.","image":"Kaiser Wilhelm I. .JPG","birth":1797.2191780821918,"death":1888.1857923497269},"Q151826":{"label":"Frederick I of Prussia","description":"1657 – 1713, Elector of Brandenburg and Duke of Prussia in personal union (Brandenburg-Prussia)","enwiki":"Frederick I of Prussia","summary":"Frederick I (German: Friedrich I.; 11 July 1657 – 25 February 1713), of the Hohenzollern dynasty, was (as Frederick III) Elector of Brandenburg (1688–1713) and Duke of Prussia in personal union (Brandenburg–Prussia). The latter function he upgraded to royalty, becoming the first King in Prussia (1701–1713). From 1707 he was also Prince of Neuchâtel.","image":"Antoine Pesne; Frederik I von Preußen.jpg","birth":1657.5232876712328,"death":1713.150684931507},"Q152229":{"label":"Friedrich Wilhelm II of Prussia","description":"King of Prussia from 1786 to 1797 (1744–1797)","enwiki":"Frederick William II of Prussia","summary":"Frederick William II (German: Friedrich Wilhelm II.; 25 September 1744 – 16 November 1797) was King of Prussia from 1786 until his death in 1797. He was also Prince-elector of Brandenburg and Prince of Neuchâtel. \nAs a defensive reaction to the French Revolution, Frederick William II ended the German Dualism between Prussia and Austria. Domestically, he turned away from the enlightened style of government of his predecessor and introduced a tightened system of censorship and religious control.","image":"Frederick William II (1744-1797).jpg","birth":1744.7322404371585,"death":1797.8739726027397},"Q2677":{"label":"Wilhelm II","description":"as King of Prussia last German Emperor from 1888 to 1918 (1859–1941)","enwiki":"Wilhelm II","summary":"Wilhelm II (Friedrich Wilhelm Viktor Albert; 27 January 1859 – 4 June 1941) was the last German Emperor from 1888 until his abdication in 1918. His fall from power marked the end of the German Empire as well as the Hohenzollern dynasty's 400-year rule over Prussia.\nBorn during the reign of his granduncle Frederick William IV of Prussia, Wilhelm was the son of Prince Frederick William and Victoria, Princess Royal. Through his mother, he was the eldest of the 42 grandchildren of Queen Victoria of the United Kingdom.","image":"Kaiser Wilhelm II of Germany - 1902.jpg","birth":1859.0712328767124,"death":1941.4219178082192},"Q33550":{"label":"Frederick II of Prussia","description":"King of Prussia from 1740 to 1786 (1712-1786)","enwiki":"Frederick the Great","summary":"Frederick II (German: Friedrich II.; 24 January 1712 – 17 August 1786) was the monarch of Prussia from 1740 until his death in 1786. He was the last Hohenzollern monarch titled King in Prussia, declaring himself King of Prussia after annexing Royal Prussia from the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth in 1772. His most significant accomplishments include military successes in the Silesian wars, reorganisation of the Prussian Army, the First Partition of Poland, and patronage of the arts and the Enlightenment. Prussia greatly increased its territories and became a major military power in Europe under his rule.","image":"Friedrich der Große (1781 or 1786) - Google Art Project.jpg","birth":1712.0628415300546,"death":1786.6246575342466},"Q37134":{"label":"Frederick William III of Prussia","description":"King of Prussia from 1797 to 1840","enwiki":"Frederick William III","summary":"Frederick William III (German: Friedrich Wilhelm III.; 3 August 1770 – 7 June 1840) was King of Prussia from 16 November 1797 until his death in 1840. He was concurrently Elector of Brandenburg in the Holy Roman Empire until 6 August 1806, when the empire was dissolved.\nFrederick William III ruled Prussia during the times of the Napoleonic Wars. The king reluctantly joined the Sixth Coalition against Napoleon in the German campaign of 1813.","image":"Friedrich Wilhelm III of Prussia - Lawrence 1814-18.jpg","birth":1770.5863013698631,"death":1840.431693989071},"Q57180":{"label":"Frederick William IV of Prussia","description":"King of Prussia (1795–1861)","enwiki":"Frederick William IV","summary":"Frederick William IV (German: Friedrich Wilhelm IV.; 15 October 1795 – 2 January 1861) was King of Prussia from 7 June 1840 until his death in 1861. Also referred to as the \"romanticist on the throne\", he was deeply religious and believed that he ruled by divine right. He feared revolutions, and his ideal state was one governed by the Christian estates of the realm rather than a constitutional monarchy.\nIn spite of his conservative political philosophy, he initially pursued a moderate policy of easing press censorship, releasing political prisoners and reconciling with the Catholic population of the kingdom.","image":"Friedrich Wilhelm IV of Prussia (1847).jpg","birth":1795.786301369863,"death":1861.0027397260274},"Q58600":{"label":"Frederick III, German Emperor","description":"as King of Prussia German Emperor (1831–1888)","enwiki":"Frederick III, German Emperor","summary":"Frederick III (Friedrich Wilhelm Nikolaus Karl; 18 October 1831 – 15 June 1888), or Friedrich III, was German Emperor and King of Prussia for 99 days from 9 March 1888 until his death in June that year, during the Year of the Three Emperors.\nKnown informally as \"Fritz\", he was the only son of Emperor Wilhelm I and was raised in his family's tradition of military service. Following the unification of Germany in 1871 his father, then King of Prussia, became German Emperor. Upon Wilhelm's death at the age of ninety on 9 March 1888, the thrones passed to Frederick, who had been German Crown Prince for seventeen years and Crown Prince of Prussia for twenty-seven years.","image":"Emperor Friedrich III.png","birth":1831.7945205479452,"death":1888.4535519125684}},"media":{"Flag of the Kingdom of Prussia (1803-1892).svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Prussia_%281803-1892%29.svg/330px-Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Prussia_%281803-1892%29.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Prussia_(1803-1892).svg","author":"Drawing created by David Liuzzo","license":"Attribution"},"Coat of Arms of the Kingdom of Prussia 1873-1918.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Coat_of_Arms_of_the_Kingdom_of_Prussia_1873-1918.svg/330px-Coat_of_Arms_of_the_Kingdom_of_Prussia_1873-1918.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_Arms_of_the_Kingdom_of_Prussia_1873-1918.svg","author":"Glasshouse, using elements by Sodacan, Katepanomegas and Regicollis","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},"Flag of Prussia (1892-1918).svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Flag_of_Prussia_%281892-1918%29.svg/330px-Flag_of_Prussia_%281892-1918%29.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Prussia_(1892-1918).svg","author":"Drawing created by David Liuzzo","license":"Attribution"},"Flag of the Kingdom of Prussia (1750-1801).svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Prussia_%281750-1801%29.svg/330px-Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Prussia_%281750-1801%29.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Prussia_(1750-1801).svg","author":"Drawing created by David Liuzzo","license":"Attribution"},"Flag of the Kingdom of Prussia (1701-1750).svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Prussia_%281701-1750%29.svg/330px-Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Prussia_%281701-1750%29.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Prussia_(1701-1750).svg","author":"Drawing created by David Liuzzo","license":"Attribution"},"Middle Arms of the Kingdom of Prussia 1873.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Middle_Arms_of_the_Kingdom_of_Prussia_1873.svg/330px-Middle_Arms_of_the_Kingdom_of_Prussia_1873.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Middle_Arms_of_the_Kingdom_of_Prussia_1873.svg","author":"Glasshouse using elements from Sodacan","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},"Graf Leopold von Caprivi cropped2.jpg":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/10/Graf_Leopold_von_Caprivi_cropped2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Graf_Leopold_von_Caprivi_cropped2.jpg","author":"not specified","license":"Public domain"},"Bundesarchiv Bild 183-1988-0113-500, Franz v. Papen (cropped)(2).jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-1988-0113-500%2C_Franz_v._Papen_%28cropped%29%282%29.jpg/330px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-1988-0113-500%2C_Franz_v._Papen_%28cropped%29%282%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-1988-0113-500,_Franz_v._Papen_(cropped)(2).jpg","author":"UnknownUnknown","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0 de"},"Hermann Göring - Röhr.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Hermann_G%C3%B6ring_-_R%C3%B6hr.jpg/330px-Hermann_G%C3%B6ring_-_R%C3%B6hr.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hermann_G%C3%B6ring_-_R%C3%B6hr.jpg","author":"Robert Röhr","license":"Public domain"},"Bundesarchiv Bild 183-B0527-0001-020, Kurt von Schleicher.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-B0527-0001-020%2C_Kurt_von_Schleicher.jpg/330px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-B0527-0001-020%2C_Kurt_von_Schleicher.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-B0527-0001-020,_Kurt_von_Schleicher.jpg","author":"UnknownUnknown","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0 de"},"Bundesarchiv Bild 102-10131, Otto Braun.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-10131%2C_Otto_Braun.jpg/330px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-10131%2C_Otto_Braun.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-10131,_Otto_Braun.jpg","author":"UnknownUnknown","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0 de"},"Paul Hirsch um 1920.jpg":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/73/P